Warlord's Lore
Void Archmage
The Archmage was once a scholar of unmatched brilliance among the Lightborne, driven to master the deepest roots of magic. But it was The Null Hour who whispered to him in the depths of his research, showing him fragments of forbidden knowledge that he believed came from the Void itself calling to him. The manipulations were so subtle that he never suspected the trap.
To gain the ultimate power he thought he desired, he sacrificed his mortal flesh to become a vessel of pure void energy. The transformation granted him limitless magical prowess, but left his form fragile as shadow. In that moment of rebirth, most of his memories were burned away—including crucial parts of the Lightborne's fragment of the Necronomium that he had once studied.
When the Lightborne discovered his corruption and exiled him, he led the Void armies in what became known as the Great Betrayal. His forces devastated the Lightborne ranks, destroyed their mage councils, and claimed Nightblade as their own. But The Null Hour had foreseen what the Archmage could not—the coming of the Attack Moon. When that impossible construct of light and paradox descended upon them, his fragile form nearly shattered under the magical strain required to fend it off. Though he succeeded in damaging it and forcing its retreat, the effort left him permanently weakened, his body barely able to contain the void energies that course through him.
Now he wields power beyond mortal comprehension, but at the cost of everything he once was. His fragile vessel requires constant vigilance—one moment of overexertion could see his essence scattered to the winds. He has become the supreme weapon of the Void, yet also its most vulnerable asset, a living reminder that ultimate power demands the ultimate price.

The Null Hour
Forged in silence where the Void devours all light, The Null Hour rose as its chosen vessel with the gift of foresight beyond mortal grasp—to behold kingdoms before their birth, wars before their spark, and the ruin of empires long before the first stone fell.
Among all the futures he witnessed, before anyone knew the ruins of Aeons, one vision haunted him above all others: the reassembly of the Silver Key and the awakening of Yog-Sothoth, the mad god whose laughter would unravel reality itself. He saw worlds consumed not by darkness or light, but by pure chaos—existence itself becoming meaningless as the Outer God devoured causality. This apocalyptic future drove every manipulation, every scheme, every calculated move.
It was he who lured the brilliant Archmage to darkness, whispering forbidden knowledge that the scholar believed came from the Void's own call. His greatest manipulation was making the Archmage think his corruption was destiny rather than design. When the scholar transformed into the Void Archmage, The Null Hour knew what would come—the Lightborne would retaliate with their ultimate weapon.
He urged the newly transformed Archmage to prepare defenses against the Attack Moon, but the Archmage laughed and said that how the Lightborne never will possess such impossible technology. He dismissed the warnings until that stellar fortress descended from the heavens, bringing the combined wrath of light and paradox. When the Archmage's fragile form began to buckle under the magical strain needed to counter such power, The Null Hour and the Voidmages worked desperately to stabilize his essence. They succeeded in driving back the Attack Moon, but the victory came at great cost—the Archmage's body remains wounded, his power forever limited lest his vessel shatter completely.
The Void's true purpose is not conquest or destruction, but prevention. They seek the Silver Key fragments not to use them, but to ensure they remain forever scattered. In The Null Hour's vision, only by keeping the Key incomplete can reality itself survive. He is neither savior nor destroyer, but the guardian of existence itself—cursed to witness the world's fate before it arrives, and to stand watch at the fragile edge between what must be and what must never come to pass.

The Dark Paladin:
When he was young and trained among the other soldiers, he was always lagging behind because he couldn't wield a sword properly. All the other soldiers laughed at his clumsy attempts, but he never faltered in his determination. If he couldn't strike down enemies, he decided, then he would become an unbreakable shield for his allies. That resolve began his journey with shield and defensive arts.
Years of relentless training transformed him from a laughingstock into something far greater—a hulking figure of hope on the battlefield, respected by all who fought under his protection. His massive frame became synonymous with safety, his presence alone enough to steady wavering lines and give courage to the fearful. Yet through all his triumphs, he kept the Forsaken Branch close, a reminder of his humble beginnings and the pain that forged his purpose.
When the Great Betrayal began, he and the Archmage fought their way to the Lightborne's backlines where The Starchild commanded her forces. The moment of ultimate victory was at hand, but the Archmage's fragile form needed a divine sacrifice to channel the Memory Severing Ray without destroying himself. In that crucial moment, the Dark Paladin made his choice—he offered up the Forsaken Branch, the symbol of his past struggles, to fuel the weapon that would claim Nightblade for the Void.
The sacrifice succeeded, but victory brought new concerns. Though Nightblade serves the Void with deadly efficiency, the Dark Paladin remains deeply skeptical of their newest weapon. He knows that true void energy does not flow through Nightblade's veins—only stolen memories and imposed loyalty. The Dark Paladin watches him carefully, knowing that all it would take is a single recovered flashback to turn their greatest asset into their most dangerous enemy. Until that day, he stands ready to be the shield that protects the Void from threats within and without.

Nightblade
He is fragments of what he once was. The deep memories remain—the feel of steel, the silence of the hunt, the certainty of purpose—but everything else burns like smoke when he tries to grasp it. The records speak of what he was before, but those days are shadow now.
During the Great Betrayal, when darkness sought to claim the light itself, he stepped between annihilation and something precious. The Archmage's Memory Severing Ray—a weapon of pure forgetting—was meant for another, someone whose loss would have plunged all creation into eternal night. In that moment, he chose sacrifice over victory, taking the full force of erasure upon himself.
When he woke, he was hollow but not empty. The Void claimed him as their perfect weapon, and he serves that role with deadly precision. Yet when he faces certain enemies in battle—a radiant figure whose light seems familiar, a warrior whose movements echo something lost—his blade hesitates for just a heartbeat. In those moments, he touches the white sheath at his side and wonders what he chose to forget.
The Void made him their assassin, but they could not kill what lives deeper than memory. In the silence after each kill, he hears echoes of a name he once answered to, sees glimpses of faces he once protected. The Starchild, The Dawnbreaker—when he fights them, something buried stirs, a recognition that cuts deeper than any blade.
He is Nightblade now, the shadow's perfect servant. But in his dreams, he remembers light.

MK (Minuscule Knight) Lore:
They used to call me a warlord, The Mechanical Genius! But alas, those were the days when my name alone could shatter enemy formations, when my tactical brilliance was spoken of in whispered reverence across the void-touched worlds. I commanded from the front lines, my cannons speaking death to all who opposed us. I was magnificent.
Then Nightblade came.
The duel was inevitable—two apex predators cannot share the same hunting ground. I had expected him to be skilled, but what I faced was something beyond mortal expertise. His blade moved like liquid shadow, each strike placed with surgical precision. When my Void-Breaker Cannon finally found its mark, I thought victory was mine. But victory became defeat in the space between heartbeats, and I found myself broken on the battlefield, my command stripped away.
Now they call me "Minuscule Knight," a bitter joke that cuts deeper than any weapon. But let them laugh—I remember the Great Betrayal, when my cannons carved through Lightborne ranks like paper. I remember the moment my Void-Breaker Cannon found that presumptuous False Oracle at the Battle of Shifting Sands, leaving her with a scar that phases in and out of existence—a permanent reminder of my precision. That foolish Null Hour, he said that never attack the Paradoxians no matter what before taking Lightborne's key piece and Necronomium, is he underestimating the might of the MK himself? I thought he could predict the future or something and will forsee my awe-inspiring victory!
Most of all, I remember my crucial role in the Great Betrayal itself. When the Void Archmage needed to maintain the siege lines while preparing his ultimate gambit against The Starchild, it was my tactical expertise that held our forces together. My cannon emplacements, my troop movements, my unflinching resolve—all were vital to that glorious campaign that brought the Lightborne to their knees.
Nightblade might have taken my glory, but they could not diminish what I accomplished. I am MK now, but I am still the architect of their greatest victory. And when the final battle comes, they will remember why they once called me Warlord and Nightblade himself will kneel before me.

Void Negromancer Lore:
Once, he was nothing more than a nameless Voidmage — one among hundreds, an apprentice to the shadowed mysteries of the abyss, with no destiny beyond servitude and minor spellcraft. His life changed during the ill-fated exploration of the Ancient Ruins of the Aeons, where time itself folds in on broken echoes of civilizations long erased. Deep within those ruins, he stumbled upon the Tomb of Gaotama Sutama, a figure whispered of even in the Void as a forbidden legend: the first Necromancer, a woman who bent the laws of life and death until the gods themselves struck her name from existence.
There, upon a throne of bone and shadow, lay her personal Grimoire of Black Eternity — a tome said to have been written in the lifeblood of stars. Against every warning carved into the walls, he opened it and read. What he saw were not mere words, but living incantations that clawed into his mind, carving the shapes of forbidden knowledge into his soul.
From that moment, he was no longer a mere Voidmage. He was reborn as the Void Necromancer. With his newfound arts, he calls the dead to rise again, their husks bound in chains of voidlight, their eyes burning with unholy fire. To him, death is no longer an end but a resource — a canvas upon which he sculpts his armies anew.
Some whisper that each resurrection costs him a piece of his own essence, and that one day he will become the very lich-king he serves to emulate. Others fear he has already surpassed Sutama, and that the Paradox of life and death itself will unravel beneath his hand. He does not care. For in his voice, the grave is silent, and in his presence, the dead obey.

God Engine Lore:
Dreadnut (Voidhollow God Machine)
Forged in the tombs of N’kai, the Dreadnut was built not as a beacon, but as a vessel of annihilation. Its body is a hollow fortress, inscribed with the Necronomium’s prophecy of “The Fang that Devours Memory.”
Its Silver Key shard does not shine — it consumes. It drinks warmth, sound, and even history from the world around it. To behold the Dreadnut is to risk being forgotten, erased from records and memory alike.
